// ALG1 Unit 04 Unit Assessment
// Unit: Functions

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q01::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q01] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1]
Which relation is a function?
{
=\((1,3), (2,5), (3,7)\)#Correct. Each input appears once and has exactly one output.
~\((1,3), (1,5), (2,7)\)#Input 1 has two outputs, so this is not a function.
~\((2,4), (2,4), (2,6)\)#Input 2 is paired with more than one output.
~\((3,1), (4,1), (3,2)\)#Input 3 has two different outputs.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q02::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q02] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.2]
If \(f(x)=4x-3\), what is \(f(5)\)?
{
~18#This is close, but \(4(5)-3\) is \(20-3\), not 18.
~20#This stops after multiplying and leaves out the -3.
=17#Correct. \(f(5)=4(5)-3=17\).
~23#This adds 3 instead of subtracting 3.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q03::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q03] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1]
What does \(f(6)=14\) mean?
{
~The input is 14 and the output is 6.#This reverses the meaning of the notation.
=When the input is 6, the output is 14.#Correct. Function notation names the output for a given input.
~The function always equals 14.#This statement only gives the output for one input.
~The function has a zero at 6.#A zero would mean \(f(6)=0\).
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q04::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q04] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.2]
The table shows \(x: 0, 1, 2, 3\) and \(g(x): 8, 10, 12, 14\). What is \(g(2)\)?
{
=12#Correct. The output paired with x = 2 is 12.
~2#This is the input, not the output.
~10#This output is paired with x = 1.
~14#This output is paired with x = 3.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q05::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q05] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
What is the domain of \((2,9), (4,13), (6,17)\)?
{
~{9, 13, 17}#These are outputs, so they form the range.
={2, 4, 6}#Correct. The domain is the set of input values.
~{2, 9, 4, 13, 6, 17}#This combines inputs and outputs.
~{2, 6, 9, 17}#This mixes inputs and outputs and omits values.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q06::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q06] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A graph crosses the x-axis at \(x=9\). What feature is \(x=9\)?
{
~y-intercept#The y-intercept occurs when x = 0.
=zero#Correct. A zero is an input where the output is 0.
~maximum#An x-axis crossing does not identify the highest output.
~starting value#The starting value is usually the output when input is 0.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q07::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q07] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.8]
What is the minimum value of \(y=|x-4|\)?
{
=0#Correct. Absolute value measures distance, and the smallest distance is 0.
~4#The graph is centered at x = 4, but the output there is 0.
~-4#Absolute value outputs are never negative.
~8#This can be an output, but it is not the minimum.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q08::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q08] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.4]
Which table matches \(y=3x+2\)?
{
~x: 0,1,2; y: 3,5,7#This starts at 3 and increases by 2.
~x: 0,1,2; y: 2,4,6#This starts at 2 but increases by 2.
=x: 0,1,2; y: 2,5,8#Correct. Substitution gives 2, 5, and 8.
~x: 0,1,2; y: 0,3,6#This matches \(3x\), not \(3x+2\).
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q09::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q09] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.4]
Function A is \(A(x)=5x+1\). Function B is \(B(x)=2x+9\). Which has the greater rate of change?
{
~Function B#Function B has slope 2, which is less than 5.
=Function A#Correct. Function A has slope 5, and Function B has slope 2.
~They have the same rate.#The slopes are different.
~Cannot be determined.#Both equations show the rates.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q10::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q10] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1]
Which mapping is not a function?
{
~1 -> 4, 2 -> 5, 3 -> 6#Each input has one output, so this is a function.
=1 -> 4, 1 -> 7, 2 -> 8#Correct. Input 1 has two different outputs.
~0 -> 3, 1 -> 3, 2 -> 3#Different inputs may share the same output.
~5 -> 9, 6 -> 10, 7 -> 11#Each input has exactly one output.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q11::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q11] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.2]
If \(h(x)=x^2+2\), what is \(h(4)\)?
{
~10#This treats \(4^2\) as 8. Squaring means \(4\cdot4\).
~16#This stops after squaring and leaves out +2.
=18#Correct. \(h(4)=4^2+2=16+2=18\).
~36#This squares \(4+2\), but the exponent applies only to x.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q12::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q12] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
The range of \((1,5), (2,5), (3,7)\) is:
{
~{1, 2, 3}#These are inputs, so they form the domain.
~{5, 5, 7}#A set lists repeated values only once.
~{1, 2, 3, 5, 7}#This combines domain and range.
={5, 7}#Correct. The outputs are 5 and 7, with repeated 5 listed once.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q13::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q13] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A function changes from increasing to decreasing at \((3,20)\). What feature is shown?
{
=maximum#Correct. A change from increasing to decreasing gives a highest point.
~minimum#A minimum occurs when a graph changes from decreasing to increasing.
~zero#A zero has output 0, but this point has output 20.
~y-intercept#A y-intercept occurs when x = 0.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q14::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q14] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.4]
A $12 fee plus $4 per item is modeled by which equation?
{
~C(x)=12x+4#This reverses the fixed fee and per-item rate.
=C(x)=4x+12#Correct. The starting cost is 12 and each item adds 4.
~C(x)=16x#This treats the fee and item cost as one rate.
~C(x)=4x-12#The fee is added, not subtracted.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q15::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q15] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.4]
Function A has slope 3 and y-intercept 8. Function B has slope 6 and y-intercept 2. Which statement is true?
{
~Function A has greater slope and greater y-intercept.#Function A has the greater y-intercept but not the greater slope.
~Function B has greater y-intercept and greater slope.#Function B has the greater slope but not the greater y-intercept.
=Function B has greater slope, and Function A has greater y-intercept.#Correct. 6 > 3 and 8 > 2.
~The functions have the same features.#Both features are different.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q16::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q16] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.8]
For \(y=|x+6|\), where is the vertex?
{
~(6, 0)#The expression is zero when x = -6.
~(0, 6)#This is the y-intercept, not the vertex.
~(0, -6)#Absolute value outputs are not negative.
=(-6, 0)#Correct. \(|x+6|=0\) at x = -6.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q17::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q17] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1]
Which statement describes a function?
{
=Every input has exactly one output.#Correct. This is the defining feature of a function.
~Every output has exactly one input.#Different inputs can share the same output.
~Every input must equal its output.#Functions do not require matching input and output values.
~The graph must be a straight line.#Functions can be nonlinear.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q18::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q18] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.2]
If \(p(x)=20-3x\), what is \(p(5)\)?
{
~17#This subtracts 3 once instead of \(3(5)\).
~35#This adds \(3(5)\) instead of subtracting.
=5#Correct. \(p(5)=20-15=5\).
~60#This multiplies 20 by 3 and ignores the input rule.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q19::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q19] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
A reasonable domain for the number of tickets purchased is:
{
~all real numbers#Ticket counts cannot be negative or fractional in this context.
=whole numbers 0, 1, 2, 3, ...#Correct. Ticket counts are nonnegative whole numbers.
~negative integers#A person cannot buy a negative number of tickets.
~decimals between 0 and 1#Ticket counts are not fractional in this model.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q20::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q20] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A y-intercept of 15 in a cost function means:
{
~the cost is always 15#A y-intercept is a starting value, not every output.
~the cost increases by 15 each item#That describes a rate of change.
~the function has a zero at 15#A zero is an input where output is 0.
=the starting cost is 15#Correct. The y-intercept is the output when input is 0.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q21::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q21] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.4]
If \(g(x)=f(x)+5\), how do outputs of \(g\) compare to outputs of \(f\)?
{
=They are 5 greater.#Correct. Adding 5 outside the function increases every output by 5.
~They are 5 less.#That would match \(f(x)-5\).
~They are multiplied by 5.#That would match \(5f(x)\).
~The inputs are 5 greater.#The change is to outputs, not inputs.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q22::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q22] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.4]
Function A is \(A(x)=2x+10\). Function B is \(B(x)=4x+1\). Which has greater output at \(x=3\)?
{
~Function B#\(B(3)=13\), which is less than \(A(3)=16\).
=Function A#Correct. \(A(3)=16\), and \(B(3)=13\).
~They are equal.#The outputs are not equal.
~Cannot be determined.#Both equations are given.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q23::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q23] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.8]
What does \(y=|x-9|\) measure?
{
~the sum of x and 9#The expression uses distance from 9, not addition.
~the product of x and 9#Absolute value is not multiplication.
=the distance between x and 9#Correct. \(|x-9|\) measures how far x is from 9.
~only values greater than 9#Inputs less than 9 also have distances from 9.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q24::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q24] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1]
Which table represents a function?
{
~x: 1,1,2; y: 3,4,5#Input 1 has two outputs.
~x: 2,2,2; y: 6,7,8#Input 2 has multiple outputs.
~x: 4,5,4; y: 1,2,3#Input 4 has two outputs.
=x: 0,1,2; y: 7,7,7#Correct. Each input has exactly one output, even if outputs repeat.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q25::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q25] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.2]
If \(q(x)=\frac{x}{2}+7\), what is \(q(12)\)?
{
=13#Correct. \(12/2+7=6+7=13\).
~10#This divides 12 by 2 but adds 4 instead of 7.
~19#This adds 12 and 7 before dividing.
~31#This multiplies by 2 instead of dividing by 2.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q26::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q26] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
A container holds from 0 to 50 gallons. Which range is reasonable?
{
~all real numbers#The amount cannot be negative or exceed 50 gallons.
=0 to 50 gallons, inclusive#Correct. The container can be empty, full, or between those amounts.
~50 gallons or more#Amounts above 50 exceed the capacity.
~only 50 gallons#The container can hold less than its maximum.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q27::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q27] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A graph is flat from \(x=2\) to \(x=6\). What behavior is shown?
{
~increasing#A flat graph does not rise.
~decreasing#A flat graph does not fall.
=constant#Correct. The output stays the same on a flat interval.
~undefined#A flat graph still has defined output values.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q28::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q28] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.4]
Which equation matches a line with slope -3 and y-intercept 4?
{
~y=4x-3#This reverses the slope and intercept.
~y=3x+4#The slope should be negative.
~y=-4x+3#This uses the wrong slope and intercept.
=y=-3x+4#Correct. The coefficient is -3 and the constant is 4.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q29::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q29] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.4]
Function A has outputs 4, 9, 14 for inputs 0, 1, 2. Function B is \(B(x)=6x+1\). Which has greater rate of change?
{
=Function B#Correct. Function A increases by 5, while Function B has slope 6.
~Function A#Function A's rate 5 is less than Function B's rate 6.
~They have the same rate.#The rates are 5 and 6.
~Cannot be determined.#The table and equation show the rates.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q30::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q30] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.8]
Which statement is true about \(y=|x|\)?
{
~The output is always negative.#Absolute value outputs are never negative.
=The minimum output is 0.#Correct. The smallest value of \(|x|\) is 0.
~The graph is horizontal.#The graph is V-shaped.
~There is no vertex.#The vertex is at \((0,0)\).
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q31::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q31] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1]
The notation \(C(4)=28\) for a cost function means:
{
~4 dollars cost 28 items#This reverses the input and output.
~the cost is always 28#This is only one input-output pair.
=4 items cost 28 dollars#Correct. The input is 4 items, and the output is 28 dollars.
~the cost per item is 28 dollars#The function value is total cost, not necessarily unit rate.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q32::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q32] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.2]
If \(r(x)=3(x+2)\), what is \(r(4)\)?
{
~14#This multiplies first and adds 2 after, but the parentheses must be evaluated first.
~20#This uses \(4+2=6\) but adds extra after multiplying.
~12#This leaves out the +2 inside the parentheses.
=18#Correct. \(r(4)=3(4+2)=18\).
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q33::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q33] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
For points \((-1,4), (0,2), (1,0)\), what is the range?
{
= {4, 2, 0}#Correct. The range is the set of output values.
~{-1, 0, 1}#These are input values, so they form the domain.
~{-1, 4, 0, 2, 1}#This mixes inputs and outputs and omits one value.
~{0, 1, 2, 4}#This includes an input value and misses -1 from the domain, but the question asks for range.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q34::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q34] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A function changes from decreasing to increasing at \((-2,5)\). What feature is shown?
{
~maximum#A maximum occurs when increasing changes to decreasing.
~zero#A zero would have output 0.
=minimum#Correct. Decreasing then increasing creates a lowest point.
~y-intercept#A y-intercept would have input 0.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q35::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q35] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.4]
Which context matches \(S(h)=18h\)?
{
~A worker starts with $18 and earns $1 per hour.#That would be \(h+18\).
~A worker earns $18 total no matter the hours.#That would be a constant function.
=A worker earns $18 per hour.#Correct. Total earnings are 18 times the number of hours.
~A worker loses $18 per hour.#The coefficient is positive, so earnings increase.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q36::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q36] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.4]
Function A is \(A(x)=3x+7\). Function B is \(B(x)=3x-2\). Which statement is true?
{
~Function B has greater slope.#Both functions have slope 3.
~Function B has greater y-intercept.#-2 is less than 7.
~The functions have different rates of change.#Both have the same rate of change.
=Function A has the greater y-intercept, and both have the same slope.#Correct. Both slopes are 3, and 7 is greater than -2.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q37::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q37] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.8]
For \(y=|x-2|\), which input gives output 0?
{
=2#Correct. \(|x-2|=0\) when x = 2.
~-2#Substituting -2 gives \(|-4|=4\).
~0#Substituting 0 gives \(|-2|=2\).
~4#Substituting 4 gives \(|2|=2\).
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q38::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q38] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.2]
The table gives \(m(0)=6\), \(m(1)=11\), and \(m(2)=16\). What is \(m(1)\)?
{
~0#This is an input value, not the requested output.
~6#This is \(m(0)\), not \(m(1)\).
~16#This is \(m(2)\), not \(m(1)\).
=11#Correct. The table pairs input 1 with output 11.
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q39::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q39] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.4]
If \(h(x)=2f(x)\), and \(f(3)=7\), what is \(h(3)\)?
{
~9#This adds 2 instead of multiplying by 2.
~5#This subtracts 2 from the output.
~21#This multiplies by 3 instead of 2.
=14#Correct. \(h(3)=2f(3)=2(7)=14\).
}

::ALG1_U04_UA_Q40::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_UA_Q40] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.4]
Function A starts at 12 and increases by 2 each step. Function B starts at 4 and increases by 5 each step. Which has greater rate of change?
{
~Function A#Function A's rate is 2.
~They have the same rate.#The rates are 2 and 5.
~Cannot be determined.#The rates are stated in the description.
=Function B#Correct. Function B increases by 5 each step, which is greater than 2.
}
